<P>
<font face="verdana,arial,helvetica" size="2">
The year is 1978 and you are David Walker, an American astronaut who finds himself trapped on the moon after his mission’s command module and landing module were mysteriously destroyed.  In fact, Walker himself should be dead…but he finds himself very much alive but trapped inside an alien base.
<P>
The occupants of the base are embroiled in a battle of Walker, whom the rebel Grays believe is “The Ward”- the person who will contact the Nexus and obtain the technovirus which the Grey’s want to obtain.  Walker must escape the moon and make his way to an abductee colony on Mar’s where many answers await him.
<P>
I should have known that the installation of this game would go smoothly when I saw that it was contained on two disks, rather than the usual four or five.  The opening movie was fun to watch with the graphics and sound coming together quite nicely.
<P>
Controlling Walker is EASY because it requires only the mouse… The left mouse interacts with the screen- pointing the cursor to where you want to move and left clicking will move Walker.  The right button is used to bring up the inventory, or to send an object to the inventory.  
<P>
Playing the game was made even more flexible by the inclusion of an “Easy” mode which allows you to skip the logical puzzles that are scattered throughout the game.  If you still need help, you can go to fragilebits website where they have posted a walk through of the game.
<P>
I was impressed with the graphics and sound suite within the game and I don’t know if it was biased by the fact that I really enjoyed the opening movie so much…although the laser fight within the moonbase is pretty good too.
<P>
Overall, if you are looking for a science fiction oriented action game you really can’t go wrong with this one.  It combines action with just enough mental challenges that you won’t get bored easily.
